/*
 * Copyright (c) 2026 Alp Lab AB
 * SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
 *
 * SD Host Controller overlay for the E1M-AEN801 (E8, M55-HE) -- E1M-EVK
 * 2626-R2 ONLY. `sdhc0` stays DISABLED on this board (the shared SoC dtsi's
 * own default, zephyr/dts/alif/ensemble_e8_peripherals.dtsi): the maintainer
 * decided (#2051) to keep SD off entirely on 2626-R2, not merely to leave
 * the mux ENABLE undriven.
 *
 * WHY DISABLED, NOT JUST MUX-UNDRIVEN: the 74LVC157 SDIO mux (U38/U39) has
 * NO high-impedance state -- with `/E` HIGH its Y outputs are forced LOW,
 * not released. U38/U39's outputs are the SoC-side nets (E1M_CLK, E1M_CMD,
 * E1M_D3..D0, E1M_SDIO_RST), so those nets are actively held low by the mux
 * *whenever the mux is powered*, regardless of `/E`. `sdhc_dwc_init()`
 * applying SD pinctrl and `set_def_config()` starting the 400 kHz
 * identification clock on P14_1 -- which happens at POST_KERNEL, before
 * main() ever runs, whether or not this app touches the mux at all -- would
 * put the SoC's own 8 mA CLK/CMD drivers in a fight with the mux's held-low
 * outputs on every SD-routed pad. Leaving `sdhc0` disabled is the only way
 * to keep those pads untouched: no pinctrl applied, no SDCLK emitted, no
 * driver contention. This is a hardware defect pending a component change,
 * not a firmware workaround -- see README.md and docs/boards/e1m-evk.md.
 *
 * PAD ROUTING HISTORY (inert while `sdhc0` is disabled, kept for whenever
 * this mux is fixed and SD is re-enabled): the SD "B" pad route
 * (CLK=P14_1, CMD=P14_0, D0..D3=P13_0..P13_3) is the module's real wiring,
 * corroborated by five independent metadata sources
 * (metadata/pinmux/aen.yaml:49-64, metadata/e1m_modules/aen/from-alif.tsv:36-48,
 * metadata/e1m_modules/aen/inter-chip.tsv:41-46, docs/cc3501e-bridge.md:74)
 * plus the EVK netlist (U39 pin 12 carries E1M_CMD to E2 AH11). All of CLK,
 * CMD and D0..D3 need the pad input buffer enabled (`input-enable`) even
 * though CLK is a push-pull SoC output from the SoC's own perspective: this
 * controller's command/data state machines clock off CLK LOOPED BACK FROM
 * THE PAD, not the pre-pad internal signal, so omitting it silently kills
 * that loopback (Alif's own DFP `demo_sd.c`/`demo_sdio.c`/`demo_sd_fatfs.c`/
 * `demo_mci_mdk_cmsisrtos2.c` and vendor Linux's dts pad config `0x23` on SD
 * CLK all agree). None of this is applied while `sdhc0` is disabled; git
 * blame this file for the last revision that wired it live.
 */

/ {
	chosen {
		zephyr,flash = &itcm; /* bench RAM/SES-load; path-ref form */
		/delete-property/ zephyr,code-partition;
	};
};

/*
 * ALP-SDK DELTA (#2051), not upstream: DISABLED, on purpose, on this board
 * -- see the file header. This is the SoC dtsi's own default
 * (zephyr/dts/alif/ensemble_e8_peripherals.dtsi); the explicit override
 * here exists only so a reader does not have to go looking for why no SD
 * pinctrl or bus-limit properties are set anywhere in this file.
 */
&sdhc0 {
	status = "disabled";
};

/*
 * ALP-SDK DELTA (#2051), not upstream: this app no longer brings up the
 * CC3501E inter-chip bridge, the SE-service MHUv2 transport, or any of
 * their DT wiring (SPI1, WIFI_EN/nRESET/READY, seservice0r/seservice0s/
 * se_service) -- all previously here to reach the SD mux ENABLE and to
 * cross-check the SD 100 MHz clock source via the Secure Enclave. Neither
 * survives in src/main.c any more: the mux is never touched (see the file
 * header), and the SE clock-cycle diagnostic was investigative machinery
 * for the now-superseded clock-source-mux theory, not part of the
 * controller-level probes this app keeps. See git blame for the last
 * revision that wired any of it.
 */
